course: world
lesson: 17
title: Air and why we need it
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Air is a mixture of gases: about four fifths is nitrogen, about one fifth is oxygen, and the rest is a little argon, a tiny amount of carbon dioxide and some water vapour.
explain: People and animals breathe in air and use its oxygen to release energy from their food; they breathe out air with less oxygen and more carbon dioxide.
explain: Green plants take in carbon dioxide and, using the energy of sunlight, make their food and give out oxygen.
explain: Fire needs oxygen too: a flame goes out when its air is cut off, which is how a fire blanket smothers a fire.
explain: The layer of air around the Earth, the atmosphere, also keeps us warm and protects us from harmful rays from the Sun.
explain: For example, if you put a glass jar over a burning candle, the flame soon goes out, because it has used up much of the oxygen inside.
fact: Air is a mixture of gases, mostly nitrogen and oxygen.
fact: About one fifth of the air is oxygen.
fact: Air holds only a tiny amount of carbon dioxide, much less than one part in a hundred.
fact: We need oxygen to release energy from our food.
fact: The air we breathe out has less oxygen and more carbon dioxide and water vapour than the air we breathe in, but it still has some oxygen.
fact: Plants take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen when they make food in sunlight.
fact: Burning needs oxygen; without it a fire goes out, and a car engine, which burns fuel, needs air coming in for the same reason.
fact: The air gets thinner the higher you go, so climbers on the highest mountains may carry oxygen.
fact: Air takes up space: push an upturned glass straight down into water and the air trapped inside keeps the water out of most of the glass.
fact: Air has weight: the air in a classroom weighs more than a hundred kilograms.
fact: Most fish take in oxygen dissolved in the water through their gills.
fact: Carbon dioxide in the air helps trap heat; more of it makes the Earth warmer.
exercise: Which gas makes up most of the air? | Nitrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Water vapour
exercise: About how much of the air is oxygen? | About one fifth | About half | Almost all of it | About one hundredth
exercise: Why does our body need oxygen? | To release energy from our food | To keep our lungs cool and clean inside | To make our bones grow hard and strong | To keep our eyes wet so we can blink
exercise: Which gas do we breathe out more of than we breathe in? | Carbon dioxide | Pure oxygen gas | Nitrogen from the air | Hydrogen, the lightest
exercise: Which gas do green plants take from the air to make their food? | Carbon dioxide | Oxygen from the air | Nitrogen, the main gas | Hydrogen, like in balloons
exercise: Why does a candle go out under an upturned glass jar? | It uses up much of the oxygen it needs to burn | The glass makes the flame too cold | The glass blocks the light it needs | The wick runs out of wax at once
exercise: How does a fire blanket put out a small fire? | It cuts the flames off from the oxygen in the air | It makes the burning things wet | It cools the fire like ice | It blows the flames away, like blowing out a candle
exercise: Why do climbers on the highest mountains sometimes carry oxygen? | The air is thin up there, so each breath has less oxygen | There is no air at all above the clouds | Cold air contains no oxygen | Mountain air is mostly carbon dioxide
exercise: How do most fish get their oxygen? | They take oxygen dissolved in the water through their gills | They swim up to gulp air at the surface | They do not need any oxygen | They make all the oxygen they need out of the food that they eat
exercise: What does the atmosphere do for us besides giving us air to breathe? | It keeps us warm and shields us from harmful rays | It makes the Earth spin round | It holds the Moon in its place | It makes the oceans salty
exercise: Does air have weight? | Yes, the air in a classroom weighs over a hundred kilograms | No, air weighs nothing at all; it just floats around us everywhere | Only wet air has any weight | Only moving wind has weight
exercise: Why does a closed room full of people become stuffy? | People used some oxygen and added carbon dioxide and moisture | The people made the air lighter | The walls soaked up the air, so there is less of it left in the room | The lights used up the air
exercise: What happens if more and more carbon dioxide gathers in the air? | It traps more heat and the Earth gets warmer | The Earth gets colder | There is more oxygen to breathe | Nothing at all happens
exercise: Why are forests sometimes called the lungs of the planet? | Trees take in carbon dioxide and give out oxygen | Trees breathe in oxygen like we do and nothing else | Trees make the wind blow | Trees store rainwater in their leaves
test: If all green plants disappeared, what would slowly happen to the air? | Its oxygen would run low and its carbon dioxide would grow | It would get more and more oxygen | Nothing, because animals make oxygen | It would all turn into water vapour
test: Why does blowing gently on a dying campfire make it burn brighter? | It brings the fire more oxygen | Your breath is full of fuel | Your warm breath heats the wood | It cools the wood so it burns better
test: Which is true about the air you breathe out? | It still has some oxygen, but less than before | It has no oxygen in it at all | It is pure carbon dioxide | It has more oxygen than the air you breathed in
test: Why is it hard to run fast near the top of a very high mountain? | Each breath brings in less oxygen | Gravity is much stronger up there | The ground is softer up there | The air is too heavy to breathe
test: A water plant grows in a goldfish bowl near a window. How does it help the fish? | It adds oxygen to the water in the light | It keeps the water cold, like ice from a fridge | It makes the water salty, just like the sea | It warms the water up for the fish at night
test: Which shows that air takes up space? | A glass pushed upside down into water stays partly dry inside | A cup fills up when you pour water in | Ice melts in a warm room | A stone sinks to the bottom of a pond
test: Why must a car engine have air coming in? | The fuel needs oxygen to burn | The engine runs on nitrogen gas | The air keeps the seats nice and cool | Air is the only fuel that it ever uses
test: Which gas do both a candle flame and a person need? | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Nitrogen | Helium
test: Which list goes from the most to the least in the air? | Nitrogen, oxygen, carbon dioxide | Oxygen, nitrogen, carbon dioxide | Carbon dioxide, oxygen, nitrogen | Oxygen, carbon dioxide, nitrogen
test: what's air actually made of mostly | Nitrogen and oxygen | Oxygen and carbon dioxide | Mostly carbon dioxide | Mostly water vapour
test: why's the candle go out when u put a jar over it | It uses up the oxygen it needs to burn | The jar makes it too cold | The glass blocks the light it needs | The smoke pushes the flame down
test: Mia says air is mostly oxygen. true? | No, only about one fifth is oxygen; most is nitrogen | Yes, nearly all the air is oxygen | Yes, about half the air is oxygen | No, most of the air is carbon dioxide
test: my brother says the air u breathe out has no oxygen left. right? | No, it has less oxygen but still some | Yes, we use up every bit of oxygen | Yes, it's all carbon dioxide | No, it has more oxygen than the air we breathe in
exam: State the approximate proportion of the air composed of oxygen. | One fifth | One half | Four fifths | One hundredth
exam: Identify the principal constituent gas of the atmosphere. | Nitrogen | Oxygen | Carbon dioxide | Water vapour
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning exhaled air. | It contains less oxygen and more carbon dioxide than inhaled air, yet some oxygen remains | It contains no oxygen whatsoever | It contains more oxygen than inhaled air | It consists entirely of carbon dioxide, the whole of the oxygen having been consumed in the lungs
exam: Whereas a fire is deprived of oxygen, determine the consequence. | The fire is extinguished | The fire burns more fiercely | The fire is unaffected | The fire produces oxygen of its own
